  user_input {
    user_response: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Flight Control Calibration Using Established Space Game Design\n\nCalibrate STARFALL\'s mouse flight controls using established design patterns from EVERSPACE 2, STAR WARS: Squadrons, and Chorus.\n\nThese games are references for control architecture and tuning concepts. Do not claim their internal formulas or unpublished values are known.\n\n## Final Configuration\n\n| Parameter | Initial Value |\n|---|---:|\n| Aim Boundary Width | 40% |\n| Aim Boundary Height | 45% |\n| Boundary Shape | Rounded Rectangle |\n| Precision Sensitivity | 0.50x |\n| Boundary Sensitivity | 0.30x |\n| Maximum Sensitivity | 1.80x |\n| Acceleration Curve Exponent | 2.0 |\n| Angular Smoothing | 0.08 seconds |\n| Reticle Recovery | 0.12 seconds |\n\nTreat these values as an initial calibration profile.\n\n## Main Goal\n\nThe precision zone currently feels too sensitive.\n\nReduce its effective mouse-to-rotation gain.\n\nSmall mouse movements should produce small, predictable ship rotations.\n\nThe player must be able to track targets and make fine corrections without overshooting.\n\n## Control Architecture\n\nKeep these parameters independent:\n\n1. Precision Sensitivity\n2. Aim Boundary Size\n3. Beyond-Boundary Acceleration\n4. Maximum Steering Sensitivity\n5. Angular Smoothing\n6. Reticle Recovery\n\nDo not let beyond-boundary acceleration affect precision-zone sensitivity.\n\nDo not use excessive smoothing to compensate for high sensitivity.\n\n## Preserve Existing Systems\n\n- Preserve the successful Pointer Lock implementation.\n- Preserve relative mouse movement.\n- Preserve continuous input at physical screen edges.\n- Preserve the progressive steering system.\n- Keep the virtual reticle inside the configured rounded rectangle.\n- Preserve the original flight physics and camera behavior wherever possible.\n\n## Calibration\n\nInspect the complete mouse-to-rotation pipeline.\n\nCheck for duplicated sensitivity multipliers.\n\nMeasure actual mouse displacement and resulting ship angular velocity.\n\nTune the values through browser gameplay testing.\n\nIf the initial values feel too sensitive or too slow, make small controlled adjustments and report the measured results.\n\n## Final Report\n\nReport the final values, the changes made, and actual gameplay testing results.\n\nDo not change unrelated game systems."
    items {
      text: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Flight Control Calibration Using Established Space Game Design\n\nCalibrate STARFALL\'s mouse flight controls using established design patterns from EVERSPACE 2, STAR WARS: Squadrons, and Chorus.\n\nThese games are references for control architecture and tuning concepts. Do not claim their internal formulas or unpublished values are known.\n\n## Final Configuration\n\n| Parameter | Initial Value |\n|---|---:|\n| Aim Boundary Width | 40% |\n| Aim Boundary Height | 45% |\n| Boundary Shape | Rounded Rectangle |\n| Precision Sensitivity | 0.50x |\n| Boundary Sensitivity | 0.30x |\n| Maximum Sensitivity | 1.80x |\n| Acceleration Curve Exponent | 2.0 |\n| Angular Smoothing | 0.08 seconds |\n| Reticle Recovery | 0.12 seconds |\n\nTreat these values as an initial calibration profile.\n\n## Main Goal\n\nThe precision zone currently feels too sensitive.\n\nReduce its effective mouse-to-rotation gain.\n\nSmall mouse movements should produce small, predictable ship rotations.\n\nThe player must be able to track targets and make fine corrections without overshooting.\n\n## Control Architecture\n\nKeep these parameters independent:\n\n1. Precision Sensitivity\n2. Aim Boundary Size\n3. Beyond-Boundary Acceleration\n4. Maximum Steering Sensitivity\n5. Angular Smoothing\n6. Reticle Recovery\n\nDo not let beyond-boundary acceleration affect precision-zone sensitivity.\n\nDo not use excessive smoothing to compensate for high sensitivity.\n\n## Preserve Existing Systems\n\n- Preserve the successful Pointer Lock implementation.\n- Preserve relative mouse movement.\n- Preserve continuous input at physical screen edges.\n- Preserve the progressive steering system.\n- Keep the virtual reticle inside the configured rounded rectangle.\n- Preserve the original flight physics and camera behavior wherever possible.\n\n## Calibration\n\nInspect the complete mouse-to-rotation pipeline.\n\nCheck for duplicated sensitivity multipliers.\n\nMeasure actual mouse displacement and resulting ship angular velocity.\n\nTune the values through browser gameplay testing.\n\nIf the initial values feel too sensitive or too slow, make small controlled adjustments and report the measured results.\n\n## Final Report\n\nReport the final values, the changes made, and actual gameplay testing results.\n\nDo not change unrelated game systems."
    }
  }
}
