how the user\'s table provides a 1-to-1 mapping with Starfall\'s steering configuration, which revealed major code flaws! I\'m now documenting and assessing the impacts of the duplicated sensitivity multiplier and conflatation in the `mouseSensitivity` and `normalSensitivity` parameters.\n\n\n**Reconciling Sensitivity Values**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the roles and values of the parameters, especially `mouseSensitivity` and `normalSensitivity`, in relation to user settings. It seems like the existing system allows `settings.mouseSensitivity` to control the reticle\'s approach to the aim boundary, while `STEERING_CONFIG.normalSensitivity` is hardcoded at 1.0. This makes it impossible for the user to control the boundary directly via the settings panel. I\'m focusing on reconciling these values and their impact on the precision and boundary zones!\n\n\n\n**Clarifying Sensitivity Terminology**\n\nOkay, I\'m now making it explicitly clear what the term \'Base Sensitivity\' means. It was used when `precisionSensitivity` was hardcoded at `1.0x` and the system didn\'t have an independent `precisionSensitivity` parameter! I\'m focused on resolving the overlap and providing clear definitions.\n\n\\n\\n\n\nOkay, I\'m now making it explicitly clear what the term \'Base Sensitivity\' means. It was used when `precisionSensitivity` was hardcoded at `1.0x` and the system didn\'t have an independent `precisionSensitivity` parameter! I\'m focused on resolving the overlap and providing clear definitions. I\'m focusing on the bug at `AimBoundary.ts` lines 274\342\200\223291. Specifically, I\'m noting the interdependence of `boundarySensitivity` and `normalSensitivity` and its impact on the `precision` zone. I\'m taking notes on the implications.\n\n\n**Analyzing Parameter Clamping**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the implications of sensitivity clamping, specifically how it impacts parameter independence and the user\'s ability to fine-tune the system. The original code was clamping `sensitivity` between `0.5` and `2.0`. I\'m verifying the edge case and the `bSens` clamp to [0.1, `nSens`] in relation to the new requirement, which is to allow tuning to 0.20 when `0.50` is still too sensitive! I\'m taking notes on the implications.\n\n\n**Evaluating Sensitivity Parameters**\n\nI\'m now deep in the math, re-evaluating sensitivity parameters, particularly focusing on `precisionSensitivity = 0.50x`, `boundarySensitivity = 0.30x`, and `p = 2.0`. I\'m meticulously tracing the player experience with mouse input to ensure a smooth and intuitive feel, analyzing the impact of these values on both fine aiming within the boundary and beyond-boundary behaviors. I\'m focusing on the relationship between mouse movement and reticle displacement.\n\n\n**Comparing Sensitivity Models**\n\nI\'m now comparing M1 (angular rotation gain) and M2 (mouse-to-reticle gain) sensitivity models, to ensure parameter independence. With M2 scaling `delta`, angular rotation remains at a 1:1 pixel, and with the \"precision sensitivity\" applied to mouse delta, I can check against the prompt requirements. I\'m focusing on the effect on the user\'s flight experience.\n\n\n**Tracing Parameter Violations**\n\nI\'m now tracing specific violations of parameter independence that I\'ve identified. Specifically, changing `precisionSensitivity` directly impacts both the distance to the aim boundary and the acceleration rate beyond it, violating the requirement. I\'m focusing on model **M1** and the relationship between mouse displacement, virtual aim, and the resulting angular velocity, and how the sensitivity value impacts each of these.\n\n\n\n**Validating Zone Parameters**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on validating the updated parameters I\'ve calculated, especially in relation to the zones.  I\'m verifying the ranges for precision sensitivity, the boundary, the acceleration distance, and the maximum sensitivity, ensuring they function as intended. I\'m taking notes on how they affect the steering behavior and the player experience. I\'m focusing on the effect on the user\'s flight experience.\n\n\n**Examining Steering Parameters**\n\nOkay, I\'m now examining the formulas governing the boundary transition, specifically Formula F1\'s edge cases within the existing framework. I\'m focusing on the sensitivity\'s behavior as mouse input crosses the aim boundary, looking at the transition and implications of Formula F1\'s calculation and the behavior of the time constants, and taking notes on this.\n\n\n**Clarifying Sensitivity Trends**\n\nOkay, I\'m now clarifying the dip! My analysis of the sensitivity formula, specifically with `e \342\211\210 0.004`, shows that crossing the boundary causes a temporary decrease in the ship\'s turn rate from the `precisionSensitivity` of 0.50x down to a `boundarySensitivity` of 0.30x before rising again! I\'m now fully immersed in the implications. I\'m taking notes on the implications, especially why both the original design and the prompt both specified this curious `boundarySensitivity` of 0.30x.\n\n\n**Evaluating Sensitivity Relationships**\n\nOkay, I\'m now meticulously evaluating the implications of boundary values. With a focus on the new steering values and Formulas F1/F2, I\'m checking if the new boundary value, with `precisionSensitivity = 0.50x` and `boundarySensitivity = 0.30x`, represents the Zone B entry sensitivity or how `targetSteer` is at the boundary. I\'m focusing on the effect on the user\'s flight experience.\n\n\n**Analyzing Formula F1**\n\nI\'m now fully focused on Formula F1 and the progressive steering system, keeping the user\'s requirements in mind. I\'m noting that the prompt specifies independent parameters and the progressive nature of the steering system! I\'m carefully checking `evaluateProgressiveAxisSensitivity` within `AimBoundary.ts` against the prompt. I\'m focusing on why a sum is bad and a slope makes sense, and how this relates to those requirements.\n\n\n**Deconstructing the Blend**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the implications of the sensitivity blend within `AimBoundary.ts` when `excessPx <= 1e-4`. It causes the sensitivity to *decrease* as the aim boundary is approached, contradicting the intended behavior. This stems from blending `precisionSensitivity` and `boundarySensitivity` and depends on a non-independent `nSens` value of `0.50`, which breaks the promise of independence.\n\n\n**Comparing Sensitivity Ranges**\n\nI\'m now comparing the provided data to confirm boundary behavior with and without the blend. I\'m focusing on the sensitivity values with pure precision sensitivity within the entire precision zone, versus the blended values in the current system. I am checking the resulting turn rates and comparing them against the non-blended version, to compare it with the prompt.\n\n\n**Refining Zone Behavior**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the boundary\'s implications. Inside the precision zone (`excessPx <= 1e-4`), `targetSensitivity` is precisely `0.50x`. There\'s zero influence from `boundarySensitivity` or acceleration. Moving from 0 to 256px, turn rate rises monotonically to 60.2\302\260/s. At `excessPx > 0`, the sensitivity goes to `0.30x`. The `recoverySmoothing = 0.12s` feature smooths to that target. If you continue out to Zone B/C, it ramps from `0.30` through `0.50` to `1.80`.\n\n\n**Clarifying Reticle Recovery**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the relationship between \"Angular Smoothing\", \"Reticle Recovery\", and their influence on flight behavior. I see that `recoverySmoothingSec = 0.12` and I am focusing on their impact. I\'m taking notes on these implications. I\'m focusing on the effect on the user\'s flight experience.\n\n\n**Investigating Smooth Recovery**\n\nI\'m now fully focused on the implications of `recoverySmoothingSec = 0.12` and its impact on the ship\'s control. I\'m focusing on the `FlightController.ts` code, specifically the `smoothedSensX` and `smoothedSensY` values within lines 457-468. Also, I\'m checking lines 165-201 and the \"reticle recovery\" behavior when returning from beyond the boundary.\n\n\n**Analyzing Inward Return Factor**\n\nI\'m now calculating the inward excess return gain in response to that sustained hard turn! I\'m focused on what happens when the player stops moving the mouse while outside the boundary. The persistent `virtualAim` is a key feature in Pointer Lock, and essential for continuous turning, so the inward gain behavior is very important for a good user experience. I\'m focusing on the effect on the user\'s flight experience.\n\n\n**Verifying Mouse Scaling**\n\nOkay, I\'m verifying the entire mouse scaling pipeline. I\'ve re-examined `FlightController.ts` from lines 373 to 540 to identify EVERY relevant multiplier! I\'m now fully immersed in understanding how `settings.mouseSensitivity` (the pixel scale) and `precisionSensitivity` (the rotation gain) interact and the role of smoothing in all the zones.\n\n\n**Tracing Sensitivity Multipliers**\n\nI\'m now tracing every multiplier in the steering pipeline, step-by-step. I\'m focusing on the interaction of `settings.mouseSensitivity` with the other key parameters, and how they relate to the target angular velocity. This includes `precisionSensitivity`, `boundarySensitivity`, `profile.yawSpeed`, and the smoothing constants. I\'m noting the impact on flight behavior.\n\n\n**Refining Steering Time Constants**\n\nI\'m now determining how time constants relate to ship agility. I\'ve calculated angular smoothing time constants, and verified how `angularSmoothingSec` and `angularDamping` interact!  The current values align *perfectly* when `angularDamping` is tied to ship class. I\'m noting the clean relationship: heavier ships preserve their inertia due to the explicit smoothing value! I\'m now comparing and contrasting these multipliers: `settings.mouseSensitivity` with `precisionSensitivity`.\n\n\n**Calculating Sensitivity Behavior**\n\nI\'m now deep in calculations to fully flesh out behavior! By removing `mouseSensitivity` from `mouseDeltaX` and directly using `precisionSensitivity`, I can precisely map mouse movement to reticle movement and analyze angular velocities for different mouse displacements within and beyond the boundary. I\'m focusing on the effect on user experience in dogfights.\n\n\n**Evaluating Precision Curve**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the math, simulating and comparing various precision exponent values against mouse offset, for yaw calculations. I\'m crunching numbers, using the given data in the original text, to ensure the right blend of responsiveness and control within the precision zone is achieved.\n\n\n\n**Calculating Vertical Motion**\n\nI\'m now calculating vertical motion and its relationship to the precision exponent, comparing the new calculation\'s behavior with the old code\'s calculations. I\'m focusing on the effect on the user\'s flight experience. I\'m also ensuring consistent behavior with vertical movement.\n\n\n**Validating Angular Velocities**\n\nI\'m now back-checking angular velocities after identifying sensitivity improvements! I\'ve confirmed that with `precisionSensitivity = 0.50x`, a small nudge produces a precise angular adjustment, and a full boundary deflection provides appropriate turning. The non-monotonic cliff at the boundary edge is greatly reduced. I\'m focusing on the effect on user experience in dogfights. I\'ve re-examined the table, and made a few notes.\n\n\n**Clarifying Design Models**\n\nI\'m now carefully differentiating the two design models as specified, clarifying how to create the `evaluateProgressiveAxisSensitivity` function. In Design X, I\'m focusing on the behavior of `targetSensitivity` inside and outside of the boundary, ensuring a pure precision zone and monotonic turning. Design Y also has new behavior.\n\n\n\n**Addressing Sensitivity Discrepancies**\n\nI\'m now fully focused on the counterintuitive sensitivity behavior I discovered! Specifically, when crossing the boundary, the turn rate momentarily *decreases* before increasing, creating a non-monotonic response. I\'m focusing on the root cause: the `boundarySensitivity` value of 0.30, and the blend I keep seeing, to eliminate this and ensure smooth angular velocity curves.\n\n\n**Revisiting Boundary Sensitivities**\n\nI\'m now grappling with the implications of `boundarySensitivity = 0.30x`. It challenges my initial understanding, and I\'m revisiting the code to determine whether the user intends it as a \"cushion\" value before the acceleration, or if the user is describing the Zone B entry sensitivity! The answer determines how the curve should be handled.\n\n\n**Clarifying Value Ratios**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the original and desired ratios for key sensitivity parameters. I\'m focusing on the ratio between the prior `normalSensitivity` of 1.0 and the requested boundary and precision sensitivities. The original value of `boundarySensitivity = 0.35` is inconsistent with the new value. The prompt states `boundarySensitivity = 0.30x`, while `precisionSensitivity = 0.50x`. I\'m taking notes on the implications.\n\n\n**Verifying Progressive System**\n\nI\'m now confirming the user is explicitly calibrating Starfall\'s progressive steering system and not requesting a rewrite. I\'m carefully checking all six parameters to ensure they function independently within the existing three-zone design and that no blending occurs that could jeopardize that.\n\n\n**Refining Code Audit**\n\nI\'m now refining the audit of the old codebase, specifically focusing on the parameter couplings and duplicated multipliers. I\'ve documented a critical bug where `settings.mouseSensitivity` inappropriately scales mouse input before the aim boundary, creating parameter dependencies. Additionally, I\'ve identified duplicate sensitivity application. I\'m focusing on the bug at `FlightController.ts` lines 374, 414, 423, 439, 450.\n\n\n**Refining Bug Fixes**\n\nI\'m now implementing the precision sensitivity adjustments to fix those bugs! Specifically, virtual aim will integrate pure 1:1 mouse pixels, and `precisionSensitivity` will be passed directly. The old code inappropriately clamps normalSensitivity, which is now fixed! I\'m taking notes on lines 274 of `AimBoundary.ts`\n\n\\n\\n\n\n\n**Refining Code Violations**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the specific code in `AimBoundary.ts` (lines 280-287) that causes sensitivity leakage. The blending creates sensitivity dependence, violating parameter independence. My focus is on eliminating this bleed and restoring monotonic sensitivity within the precision zone. I\'m taking notes on how a `precisionSensitivity` value of 0.50x is directly tied to a value of 1e-4.\n\n\n**Validating Smooth Transition**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on verifying the sensitivity blend during boundary transitions. With `excessPx <= 1e-4`, I\'ve confirmed `targetSensitivity` equals `precisionSensitivity` (0.50x), fully independent of other parameters. However, I need to focus on behavior when `excessPx > 0`, where the smooth C\342\210\236 exponential blend starts. The transition, with a recovery smoothing of 0.12s, appears smooth, but I must validate the angular response.\n\n\n\n**Refining Independence Solution**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the implications of sensitivity clamping, specifically focusing on a proposed fix to the boundary sensitivity. Previously, `boundarySensitivity` was clamped to a value less than or equal to `precisionSensitivity`, causing the values to be linked. This is a violation of independence. My new proposed fix is to clamp `boundarySensitivity` to independent bounds.\n\n\n**Validating Zone Parameters**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the implications of sensitivity clamping, specifically focusing on how it impacts parameter independence and the user\'s ability to fine-tune the system. The original code was clamping `sensitivity` between `0.5` and `2.0`. I\'m verifying the edge case and the `bSens` clamp to [0.1, `nSens`] in relation to the new requirement, which is to allow tuning to 0.20 when `0.50` is still too sensitive! I\'m taking notes on the implications. I\'m focusing on why a sum is bad and a slope makes sense, and how this relates to those requirements.\n\n\n**Implementing Angular Smoothing**\n\nI\'m now integrating user-adjustable angular smoothing via `angularSmoothingSec`! I\'ve added the parameter to the configuration and the settings panel for control and calculated a dynamic `effectiveAngularTau`, accounting for ship inertia. This is a crucial step for achieving a more refined and responsive feel across different ship classes.\n\n\n**Validating Transition Smoothing**\n\nOkay, I\'m now verifying the 0.12-second reticle recovery! After calculations for the time constant (`0.12s` -> ~90% settled in `0.28s`) and virtual aim excess recovery, the system exhibits a smooth transition during boundary crossings. I\'m confirming that `smoothedSensX` and `smoothedSensY` transition smoothly.\n\n\n**Validating Return Gain**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the implications of sensitivity clamping, specifically focusing on the inward excess return gain! I\'m verifying how virtual aim recovers and re-enters the precision zone, especially when the mouse is stopped outside the boundary. The `inwardGain` calculation is critical, and a persistent `virtualAim` is essential for sustained turns. I\'m focusing on the behavior of `excessX` when the mouse stops moving.\n\n\n**Validating Return Edge Behavior**\n\nNow I\'m diving deeper into that inward return gain behavior, specifically after that sustained hard turn! I\'m focused on what happens when the player stops moving the mouse while outside the boundary. The persistent `virtualAim` is a key element and essential for continuous turning, so the inward gain behavior is very important for a good experience. With those values, it seems the smoothed reticle moves smoothly back into the precision zone, even during a full 180!\n\n\n\n**Verifying Smooth Transitions**\n\nI\'m now calculating reticle recovery with those time constants! I\'m verifying the 0.12-second reticle recovery to confirm a smooth transition during boundary crossings, by ensuring that `smoothedSensX` and `smoothedSensY` transition smoothly. I\'m taking notes on those implications. I\'m focusing on the effect on the user\'s flight experience. I\'ve re-examined the table, and made a few notes. I\'m also ensuring consistent behavior with vertical movement.\n\n\n**Calculating Precision Gains**\n\nI\'ve just finished calculating yaw rates within the precision zone with `precisionSensitivity` at 0.50x. At the Interceptor\'s edge, a small mouse flick yields 60.2\302\260/s yaw, and 55.9\302\260/s pitch! The original average gain across that zone is cut precisely in half! I\'m now comparing and contrasting these multipliers: `settings.mouseSensitivity` with `precisionSensitivity`.\n\n\n**Refining Yaw Calculations**\n\nI\'m now running comprehensive yaw calculations based on the provided data. With `precExp = 1.35`, a small 15px mouse flick now produces 0.5\302\260/s yaw, and a 30px flick produces 2.3\302\260/s. At 50px, it\342\200\231s 5.5\302\260/s. Vertical adjustments at those same displacements result in 1.4\302\260/s, 4.7\302\260/s, and 10.4\302\260/s pitch respectively. Finally, I\'m checking the final values in both dimensions.\n\n\n"
