  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Verifying Core System Integrity**\n\nI\'m pleased; the `CombatManager` is untouched, adhering perfectly to the directive of isolating changes to the steering and flight calibration pipeline. That was a relief. Now, I\'m deep-diving into a comprehensive check on the *entire* system to ensure nothing was overlooked.\n\n\n**Charting Parameter Integration**\n\nI\'ve got the six independent parameters fully mapped now, with their exact calculations, names, and migration methods for storage. I have browser measurement plans ready to go for verification. I\'m focusing on the UI sliders now.\n\n\n**Updating Storage Integration**\n\nI\'ve got a crucial update: I need to increment `SETTINGS_STORAGE_KEY` to prevent old, uncalibrated values from overriding the new calibrated defaults. I need to make sure we\'re bumping to `starfall.settings.v5` in `src/config/ships.ts`, while still preserving user preferences like audio volume and graphics settings from previous versions.\n\n\n**Confirming Pipeline Integrity**\n\nI\'m confident; I\'ve meticulously checked the math in the mouse-to-rotation pipeline, and it\'s pristine. There are no duplicated multipliers, couplings, or unexpected integrations. We are in great shape for space combat. Now I am making a change to ensure every user gets the new calibrated steering while preserving their preferred settings.\n\n\n**Analyzing Aim Dynamics**\n\nI\'ve nailed down the mechanics of the virtual aim reticle. The elimination of duplicated sensitivity multipliers is a major win; it means a clean 1:1 relationship between mouse movement and reticle movement within the aim boundary. This rounded rectangle is also totally independent of precision sensitivity and beyond-boundary acceleration, a big plus.\n\n\n**Evaluating Sensitivity Expansion**\n\nI\'m now deep in `AimBoundary.ts`, evaluating progressive axis sensitivity line by line. I am focusing on the `evaluateProgressiveAxisSensitivity` function, and how it handles parameters. I\'m scrutinizing how the system maintains `(excessX, excessY)` beyond the aim perimeter, which controls acceleration outside the reticle. This ensures we\'re maximizing precision.\n\n\n**Refining Sensitivity Precision**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on sensitivity calculations within `AimBoundary.ts`, especially how we are handling the transition and progressive acceleration outside the reticle. The \"precision\" zone is independent of the other two, which is excellent. We are doing fine with excess pixels as well, and are starting to use `excessPx`, the clamping of boundary sensitivity, and the usage of `p`, `mSens`, and `Sens` to determine overall sensitivity.\n\n\n**Validating Function Invariants**\n\nI\'m verifying the independence of `targetSensitivity` as parameters change in the `evaluateProgressiveAxisSensitivity` function. Precision sensitivity adjustments within the boundary directly affect the `targetSensitivity`, but beyond the boundary, the target is unchanged. Aim boundary size and beyond-boundary acceleration also leave precision-zone sensitivity unaffected. I\'m focusing on ensuring no unintended dependencies arise.\n\n\n**Isolating Sensitivity Effects**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the effects of individual sensitivity changes. Notably, maximum steering sensitivity only affects the asymptotic ceiling outside the precision zone, precision-zone sensitivity remains untouched. Angular smoothing solely impacts the rate of turn, not the steady-state rate or geometry, and reticle recovery smoothens sensitivity recovery, but doesn\'t affect the steady-state precision. This is critical.\n\n\n**Calculating Precision Exponents**\n\nI\'m now running empirical calculations to compare `precisionExponent = 1.30` and `1.15`, to understand sensitivity impact at 1280x720. My calculations show micro-corrections are effortless. This should make distant fighters easier to target with the system. I have results for 20-256px micro-corrections.\n\n\n**Verifying Angular Velocities**\n\nI\'ve just finished a round of empirical calculations, and I\'m very satisfied! The system yields silky smooth pursuit tracking without overshooting, thanks to `medium` `64\342\200\223128 px` tracking turns at `9.4 \342\200\223 24.3 \302\260/s`. Sweeping turns inside the precision box at `256 px` are responsive, achieving `60.2 \302\260/s`. I\'ve documented the angular velocities from full precision to asymptote!\n\nI\'ve also confirmed that `maxSafeYawMultiplier` and `maxSafePitchMultiplier` are above `maxSensitivity` during testing.\n\n\\n\\n\n\n**Checking FlightController Functions**\n\nI\'m now checking `FlightController.ts`. Specifically, I am analyzing lines 235-236 and 274-275 to make sure the functions `initialize`, `reset`, and `centerAimCursor` are functioning as designed. I am paying close attention to any possible interference.\n\n\n**Refining Sensitivity Precision**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the adjustments within `FlightController.ts` after scrutinizing `AimBoundary.ts` sensitivity calculations. My goal is to ensure the `precisionSensitivity` parameter is utilized correctly. I\'m focusing on lines 235-236, 274-275, and 294-295 to make sure there\'s zero transient from the startup sensitivity of `1.0` to the actual value of `0.50`, and confirm we\'re using the new precision sensitivity value correctly in `createInitialTelemetry`. Then I\'ll check `FlightHUD.tsx` and `SettingsPanel.tsx`.\n\n\n"
